  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> The binomial theorem gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mi>n</mi><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>r</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> This is a GP beginning at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with ratio k/4. Since k is positive, convergence requires <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, allowing only k=1,2,3.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> For those values, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The denominator is positive, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math> is equivalent to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>. Thus k=2 and k=3 satisfy both conditions.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> The initial random choice is uniform over eight integers, not only over the convergent cases. The required probability is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>. Answer B.</p>"
}
